+# Bug Assessment: Preserve orders after customer deletion and fix admin pagination
+
+- **Slug**: ticket
+- **Created**: 2026-11-18T21:55:00+00:00
+- **Source**: pasted text
+- **Verdict**: valid
+- **Severity**: high
+
+## Report (verbatim or summarized)
+
+Support cannot open orders or list orders by customer after deleting a customer account. The admin order list also returns an empty result and zero total for any page after the first. Orders must remain available without a live customer, customer-id history must remain queryable, pagination must honor zero-based pages and filters, and a later registration using the old email must not inherit old orders.
+
+## Symptom
+
+Deleting a customer causes `GET /api/v1/private/orders/{id}` and `GET /api/v1/private/orders/customers/{id}` to return 404 even though the order data remains. `GET /api/v1/private/orders` does not correctly return later pages or totals. Expected behavior is to retain and expose historical orders, with a null customer object when the account no longer exists, and to scope a newly registered account to its new id.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. Create a customer and place one or more orders; record the customer and order ids.
+2. Delete the customer through `DELETE /api/v1/private/customer/{id}`.
+3. Request the order by id and request `/private/orders/customers/{id}`; both currently fail when the customer lookup is absent.
+4. Register a new customer using the deleted account's email and verify it has a different id and no historical orders.
+5. Create enough orders for multiple pages and request `/private/orders?count={n}&page=1` with and without filters; later pages should contain matching orders and preserve the full matching total.
+
+## Suspected Code Paths
+
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java:112-151` — customer-order listing loads the customer first and sends 404 when it is deleted.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/order/facade/OrderFacadeImpl.java:936-970,1107-1150` — admin/list and detail response mapping, including nullable customer lookup.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepositoryImpl.java:112-260` — admin query filters, ordering, count and pagination.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/utils/RepositoryHelper.java:24-43` — zero-based page offset and total-page calculation.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/order/Order.java:71,315-320` — orders retain `customerId` independently of the customer entity.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepository.java:8-16` — order detail query fetches order data without requiring a customer relation.
+
+## Root Cause Hypothesis
+
+Confidence: high. The order model stores the customer id and historical contact/address fields, so deleting the customer need not delete the order. The customer-order API incorrectly treats customer existence as a prerequisite instead of querying by the retained scalar id. The modern admin list path has two likely defects: its empty-result mapping drops totals and its repository pagination helper computes total pages using integer division plus one, yielding incorrect exact-boundary totals; the repository query also needs consistent grouped filter predicates so `and`/`or` combinations do not escape the store/filter scope.
+
+## Proposed Remediation
+
+**Preferred**: Remove the live-customer existence requirement from the private customer-order endpoint. Build an `OrderCriteria` with the requested customer id and delegate to the order service/facade so deleted ids still return their orders, while response population leaves `customer` null if the lookup is absent. Preserve the existing scalar customer id and order snapshot fields; do not rebind orders by email.
+
+Correct the modern order repository query and pagination path: apply all filters within grouped predicates, bind status/email/name/id consistently, use the requested zero-based page and page size, order newest first, and calculate `totalPages` as ceiling(total/pageSize). Ensure empty and out-of-range pages return an initialized empty list with the matching `recordsTotal` and `totalPages`. Add focused service/repository/API tests for deleted customers, customer-id isolation after re-registration, filtered page two, exact page boundaries, and empty customer ids.
+
+**Files likely to change**:
+
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java`
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepositoryImpl.java`
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/utils/RepositoryHelper.java`
+- Relevant order API/repository tests under `sm-shop/src/test` and `sm-core/src/test`
+
+**Tests to add or update**:
+
+- Customer-order endpoint returns historical orders and `recordsTotal` for a deleted customer id, and returns an empty list with zero for an unknown id.
+- Deleted-account order detail keeps billing/delivery/email and has no customer; status/history/payment paths remain usable.
+- Re-registration with the same email has a distinct customer id and sees no old orders.
+- Admin order listing returns zero-based later pages, newest-first order, matching totals, correct ceiling `totalPages`, and empty out-of-range pages for status/email/name/id filters.
+
+## Risks & Considerations
+
+- Historical order data must remain independent of customer email lookup; matching by email would leak old orders to a replacement account.
+- Query predicate grouping must not broaden results across merchants or unrelated filters.
+- Fetch joins over multiple collections may produce duplicate rows; count and result behavior should be covered by integration-level persistence tests.
+- Existing clients may depend on the old empty-list shape, so preserve the response fields while correcting totals and null customer behavior.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- [NEEDS CLARIFICATION: None for the requested behavior; database-backed integration coverage is preferred if the test fixture can create the required order graph.]
